Local TTS is a tool for running open text-to-speech models in WordPress. It does not contain any
voice model, speech data or speech runtime. Those are third-party works that the site owner chooses
and downloads from their publishers on the plugin's Voices page; they are stored under
wp-content/uploads/local-tts/ and sent to readers' browsers by the site. Each one keeps its own
licence, and the site owner is responsible for respecting it. The plugin shows each component's
licence before it is installed and writes a CREDITS.txt file (with the licence texts the publisher
provides) next to the downloaded files.


Third-party code included in the plugin
---------------------------------------

assets/js/engine/ema-lightning.js
  Adapted from ozcancelik/ema-lightning-web (Apache License 2.0), tts.js and normalizer.js,
  https://github.com/ozcancelik/ema-lightning-web (commit a9a688b19badc36486cdc2444ba957bc63ac860e),
  which port frontend.py, chunker.py and engine.py of EMA Lightning (Apache License 2.0,
  https://github.com/canberk7/ema-lightning).
  Changes: model bytes and the runtime are passed in by the worker, one PCM buffer per segment,
  cancellation checks, Turkish pre-normalisation (preTurkish).
  The Apache License 2.0 text is in licenses/Apache-2.0.txt and the upstream NOTICE in
  licenses/ema-lightning-web-NOTICE.txt. Apache-2.0 code may be combined into a GPL-3.0 work;
  the Apache terms continue to apply to the adapted portions.

assets/js/engine/piper.js
  Written for Local TTS. It follows the inference steps of diffusionstudio/vits-web (MIT),
  https://github.com/diffusionstudio/vits-web; no code is copied.


Components the site owner may download (not distributed with the plugin)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

- ONNX Runtime Web (MIT), Microsoft, https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime
- EMA Lightning Turkish voice (Apache-2.0): ONNX conversion of canberkkkkkk/ema-lightning weights,
  from ozcancelik/ema-lightning-web; normalizer.wasm is a WebAssembly build of Erdem Tuna's
  normalizer-tr (Apache-2.0), https://github.com/erdemtuna/normalizer-tr. Third-party notices for
  its Rust dependencies: licenses/normalizer-tr-THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.
- Piper phonemizer: piper_phonemize.wasm/.data from diffusionstudio/piper-wasm and the matching
  JavaScript glue from diffusionstudio/vits-web (MIT). The WebAssembly build contains eSpeak NG,
  GPL-3.0-or-later, https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng (source:
  https://github.com/rhasspy/piper-phonemize and https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng).
- Piper voices, https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices. Each voice has its own dataset
  licence, shown on its MODEL_CARD and in the admin screen. Several voices are fine-tuned from the
  "lessac" voice, whose Blizzard 2013 dataset licence may restrict commercial use.
- Any voice the site owner adds from the Piper library or from their own addresses.

The model cards ask that generated audio be disclosed as synthetic; the player says
"Reading with a synthetic voice" while it plays and can show each voice's credit line.
